    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "I propose to add support of reading configuration from `pyproject.toml` file. Why:\r\n1. it is standard\r\n2. everyone does it (black, mypy, pylint, ...)\r\n3. it is user friendly.\r\n4. can be used not only by setuptools but also meson and friends (they will basically not care since the loading of configuration will be done by cython).\r\n\r\nThis allows the project to have one central place for configuration and when the `cython` command is executed under the project root, it searches for `pyproject.toml` for configuration (this is what tools do in general). pyproject.toml can store all global configuration for the project and this prevents users from unnecessarily repeating parameters for commands. This will allow also intelligently selecting [shared module features](https://cython.readthedocs.io/en/latest/src/userguide/source_files_and_compilation.html#selecting-features-to-be-shared) - during compilation. Cython can read which features are shared and include the ones in the user modules that are not shared (instead of today's plain crashing when a user misses to include some shared feature to shared module)\r\n\r\nThe pyproject.toml can look as follows:\r\n```toml\r\n[tool.cython]\r\nshared = \"module.pyshared\"\r\n[tool.cython.options]\r\n...\r\n[tool.cython.directive]\r\n...\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe possible downside is that the toml parser was included only in Python 3.11 and later, hence, it might require a dependency for Cython for lower python versions (unless we manage to read the relevant parts of the file manually or as INI file).\r\n\r\n_Originally posted by @matusvalo in https://github.com/cython/cython/pull/7977#discussion_r3961157014_",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "_I'll repost my feedback from #7977, and then answer the new question that was posted in response before the thread moved here._\n\nReading from `pyproject.toml` definitely won't be a general solution, and we cannot use that in SciPy et al. I'm fairly certain it cannot work at all for Meson, CMake, etc. because they are completely unaware of `pyproject.toml`'s existence. It's a Python-specific file, while `cython` gets invoked from a general-purpose, Python-independent build system. Those build systems may not even have access to a TOML reader. It's not a robust idea, so I'd caution against relying on any static file and away from deterministic CLI invocations.\n\n\n> This allows project to have one central place for configuration and when `cython` command executed under project, it searches for `pyproject.toml` for configuration (this is what tools do in general).\n\nThis is not what _compilers_ and other tools during _build execution_ do. Cython also doesn't have a good history here:\n\n- the path walking for `__init__.py` files to determine module name has heuristics that turn into a surprising unwritten rule and needs explicitly working around for out-of-tree builds.\n- there's a reproducibility bug (fix coming soon) for `.pxd` path handling as well.\n\nExplicit and in a single place, `cython <flags> <input-sources> <output-sources>` is more discoverable and easier to maintain than splitting build config across multiple files. \n\nI'd recommend discarding any idea of `pyproject.toml` usage here. But if you do want to push it forward, please document it explicitly as a `setuptools`-focused convenience option only.\n\n****\n\n_@scoder asked: But would they need to know about it? As with tools like test runners, coverage.py or linters, Cython would simply read the file if it exists and extract default parameters/directives/etc. from it, which can then be overwritten on the command line or within each source file (as usual). An existing toml file would have to be findable by cython when it runs, which can be achieved by starting cython from the project base directory or by copying pyproject.toml to the build directory (although it would seem strange if build tools were unable to access the project checkout)._\n\nSince `cython` is typically not invoked from the project root and you can't make other assumptions either, the only way for `cython` to find the `pyproject.toml` is indeed for the user to tell the build system to copy `pyproject.toml` over to the build directory. Which would be very weird UX.\n\nIn addition, even if that worked, it would add a dependence of every extension module with Cython in the loop on all of `pyproject.toml`, which is very bad for incremental builds. Example: you're working on linting rules and touch `pyproject.toml` a lot for linting rules; now each time you do so you get all Cython targets rebuilt.\n\nThere might be more problems that those two, but even those two alone are disqualifying I think. ",
